 "notes": "What to check:\n1. Maidens (most important; affects 197 observations). 'South Maidens' observations run from 1882 to 1887, but stations.json has maidens-south seen only in 1887. For 1881-86 it puts the single listed 'Maidens' under maidens-north. I resolved South Maidens to maidens-south throughout, going by the name. Either maidens-south's 'seen' years are too narrow, or the earlier 'Maidens' list entry is really the south tower. Check the station lists for 1881-86.\n2. Ambiguous items, resolved as 'stations' with the possible candidates. Each is ONE station, not several, so don't count them as joint observations: n 69 (Arklow 'West'), 70 (Arran Island, no qualifier), 331, 334, 338, 1379 (Eagle Island, qualifier missing, or 'S.' which it doesn't have), 1115 (Slyne Head 1886, no qualifier). n 339 is commentary about the whole of Eagle Island, so both lights really are meant there.\n3. Eagle Island OCR. I read '#', 'H' and 'L' after 'Eagle Island,' as 'E.' (n 332, 333, 335, 336, 520-522, 655, 704). This follows the 'Hagle/Kagle/Lagle' = Eagle pattern; spot-check one or two against the page images.\n4. Unresolved: n 922 'Niiyband' (1884) and n 1057 'Rowe' (1886). Both are left as kind station with empty ids. Low-confidence guess: n 983 'Pasta!' = Fastnet.\n5. Mew Island 1882-83 (n 845): I treated it as a place because the light was still on Lighthouse Island until 1884. Some of the 5 observations may be the station's own.\n6. Places near stations that may be the keeper's remarks (Bull Rock, Cow Rock, Little Skellig, Inishvickillane, the Saltees, Corclough Lake). The observations probably belong to a neighbouring station's section; decide whether to attach them to it.\n7. St John Point (n 1198) is a real lighthouse but not in the scheme. I took it as Co. Down; Co. Donegal is also possible.\n8. Spelling noise not covered by the 'printed' aliases (Laght/Lnght/Inght-ship, fathlin, fockabill, Innshirahull, etc.) could be added as aliases.",
